<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Penang is an island and state on the north-west coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, developed by the British as a port from 1786. It became a major trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a> place it in a line of entrepôts, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, that followed the commercial model established by Srivijaya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penang.jpg" alt="Penang" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original designer of the flag is unknown. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Penang_(Malaysia).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Penang is divided into two parts by the Penang Strait, which is only 3 kilometres wide at its narrowest point. Penang Island to the west holds the capital, George Town, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-wellesley">Seberang Perai</a> lies on the mainland to the east, where the state borders Kedah and Perak. Penang Island covers 295 square kilometres and has a hilly, mostly forested interior, with Penang Hill, 833 metres high, as the state's highest point. George Town began as a small settlement at the island's northeastern tip. The name comes from the Malay Pulau Pinang, meaning areca palm island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Remains found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-wellesley">Seberang Perai</a> show that around 5,000 to 6,000 years ago nomadic Melanesians lived there in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> era. Pali inscriptions on the Cherok Tok Kun megalith suggest that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujang-valley">Bujang Valley</a> civilisation, based in what is now Kedah, held authority over parts of Seberang Perai by the 6th century. The whole of Penang later formed part of Kedah, which came under Siamese suzerainty by the late 18th century. Penang's modern history began in 1786, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-light">Francis Light</a> of the British East India Company obtained the island from Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-mukarram-shah">Abdullah Mukarram Shah</a> of Kedah in exchange for military aid.</p><p>Light founded the Port of Penang as a free port alongside George Town, and Penang later joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a>. A Royal Charter of 1807 led to the founding of a Supreme Court at Fort Cornwallis. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Japanese army took Penang without a struggle on 19 December 1941, and the British regained control in 1945. The state merged with the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, which became independent in 1957.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Penang is a state of Malaysia, linked across the strait by the Penang Bridge and the Second Penang Bridge. It is one of four Malaysian states without a hereditary monarchy, and its head of state is a Governor appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-di-pertuan-agong">King of Malaysia</a>. The unicameral State Legislative Assembly has 40 seats and meets in George Town. Its people include Chinese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>, Indians, Eurasians and Siamese. After the free port status was withdrawn in 1969, Chief Minister Lim Chong Eu set up the Bayan Lepas Free Industrial Zone in 1972, and the economy shifted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> trade to electronics manufacturing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Penang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Malaysia grew out of Britain's possessions in the Malay world: the Straits Settlements of <b>Penang</b>, Malacca and Singapore, the Malay sultanates brought under British protection between 1874 and 1914, and, in Borneo, the territories of the North Borneo Chartered Company and the Brooke Rajahs of Sarawak, which became…</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The commercial infrastructure Srivijaya developed, with dependable harbour facilities, predictable customs procedures and the protection of merchant shipping, set the pattern for the entrepôt system later followed by Malacca, Johor, <b>Penang</b> and finally Singapore.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In British Malaya, riots in <b>Penang</b> and Singapore inspired by the movement led the British to pass the Registration of Schools Ordinance.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>A British documentary of 1938, Five Faces, used it as background music, and it was played on <b>Penang</b> radio in 1939.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rasa-sayang">Rasa Sayang</a></li><li><blockquote>Light landed on <b>Penang</b> in August 1786, renamed it Prince of Wales Island and founded George Town.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-of-wales-island">Prince of Wales Island</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824 secured Dutch Malacca for Britain, the East India Company in 1826 joined it with Prince of Wales Island (<b>Penang</b>) and Singapore into a single presidency.</blockquote><a href="/state/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a></li></ul></section>
